 {:db/id "q-16645" :question/id #uuid "ba3205c4-c133-5ac7-861e-655b59a14526" :question/math-academy-id "q-16645" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)$ and $(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-16645/ma-source-field/065c55056ff6c0d4fc9324f40184ab3d34c6f7f7502b6a883eea73921758ed70"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
