 {:db/id "q-170347" :question/id #uuid "5b98dba6-8029-58a2-a81c-ae05e5ca1860" :question/math-academy-id "q-170347" :question/problem "![](images/71/71d9df0708a77639f3f7b2640ea2a03cebbfd72e6cfb3369c07d83a89eba6508.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170347/ma-source-field/e4fd06a98aef2bad7493e4fd0f626b422dd8d889acde4752c362a2023bf45444"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
