 {:db/id "q-170558" :question/id #uuid "ed5c021d-75c0-5eb0-8447-cc8817c3eb31" :question/math-academy-id "q-170558" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1$ intersects the line $y=8x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=8x,$ we first replace $y$ with $8x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{(8x)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{64{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ -3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=8x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170558/ma-source-field/0c0bc814b769c3e4f629c748638e56c523534e1813d91caabf249ec763383ce0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
