 {:db/id "q-182507" :question/id #uuid "e3eb0922-de2b-5aa7-8232-87f927107273" :question/math-academy-id "q-182507" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you exercise regularly, then you'll improve your health.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You exercise regularly.\n\n- $B:$ You will improve your health.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You do not exercise regularly.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not exercise regularly, or you will improve your health.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-182507/ma-source-field/2bd3a5ef447e0f2d1515f1be4fb820c7bd1fa031c503f348537dc3fcf87718a7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
