 {:db/id "q-182592" :question/id #uuid "98ecba05-28ef-5ea8-9d45-86a0357f6077" :question/math-academy-id "q-182592" :question/problem "$\\,$ Wear a jacket, or you will get cold.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Wear a jacket.\n\n- $B:$ You will get cold.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't wear a jacket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-182592/ma-source-field/45729b8da420bef1c9d5f38b86b5c5420eff833df732b7c1acf869e0273f0150"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
