 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "c37bd0c3-9779-5d09-a1bb-d6c4e0fab0c7"] :question/problem "$\\,$ Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not water the plants.\n\n- $B:$ The plants will grow.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You water the plants.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, then they will grow.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, they will grow.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-182595/ma-source-field/9081d68467f72394a1c2ad1c96e4cca72775be84229359fa069cf303d6704642"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
