 {:db/id "q-183467" :question/id #uuid "58bb6ca7-db5e-5a82-931b-128a7693b5ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-183467" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $n\\in N$ such that $\\frac{n}{10}\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(n):\\frac{n}{10}\\in N,\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n¬(∃n\\in N,\\,P(n))≡∀n\\in N,\\,¬P(n).\n$$\n\nFinally, $∀n\\in N,\\,¬P(n)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-183467/ma-source-field/35d2bd0b925a83b098171ca64762de20858cc8fa78c9859054f9984270b20c29"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
